"G0B04":"The goal is to send a lightning surge into the earth before it enters the house. Putting the lightning ground at the equipment would route the surge through the house. The ground rods go outside, and the station's equipment ground is bonded to them.\n\nArrestors go where the feed lines enter the building.",
"G0B05":"A ground fault circuit interrupter compares current in the hot and neutral wires. If some current is missing, it's leaking to ground, perhaps through a person, and the GFCI cuts power in milliseconds.\n\nNormal current from hot to neutral doesn't trip it.",
"G0B06":"The NEC sets standards for safe wiring, grounding and installation. It doesn't cover bandwidth, modulation or RF exposure, which are FCC matters.\n\nIt's about not starting fires or shocking anyone.",
"G0B07":"A harness only protects you if it can hold you. Check its weight rating and age, and replace it when it's past its service life.\n\nHolding on with one hand isn't the rule. Stay tied off to the tower at all times and keep three points of contact while climbing. Carry only light tools, tethered so they can't fall, and haul heavy ones up on a rope.",
"G0B08":"Lockout-tagout means switching off and locking the power, with a tag saying why, so no one can turn it back on while you're aloft.\n\nNever unground a tower.",
"G0B09":"Generators produce carbon monoxide, which is deadly and has no smell. Run one only outdoors, away from doors, windows and vents, never in a house or garage, even with the doors open.\n\nStore fuel away from the generator, and ground it as its instructions say.",
"G0B10":"Lead is toxic when swallowed. Traces on your fingers can reach your food, so wash your hands after soldering.\n\nSolder doesn't turn into poisonous gas from RF.",
"G0B11":"If grounds aren't connected, a strike can raise one to a huge voltage relative to another, and current jumps between them through your equipment. Bonding keeps them at the same potential.\n\nBends should be gentle, never sharp right angles.",
"G0B12":"An interlock switch cuts the power when a cover is removed, and some designs also short the high-voltage capacitors. Still, never assume the capacitors are discharged; filter capacitors can hold a lethal charge.\n\nIt isn't about warranties or overheating.",
"G0B13":"Placed at the entry point and connected to the outside ground system, arrestors send surges to earth before they reach your equipment.\n\nNot on the antenna, and not in series with ground leads."
